# kiosk-linux/configs/qemu_x86_64_kiosk_defconfig
#
# A minimal (sparse) defconfig for Buildroot 2024.02.9 (the exact pinned version
# this was verified against — see build.sh's BUILDROOT_TAG) — NOT a full copy of
# stock configs/qemu_x86_64_defconfig with overrides layered on top. Only the
# symbols that differ from Buildroot's own defaults are listed below;
# `make defconfig` fills in everything else from Buildroot's standard defaults.
# Compared against the stock qemu_x86_64_defconfig's own settings, the intent
# per the task-1 brief was:
#   - BR2_TOOLCHAIN_BUILDROOT_CXX=n     (no C++ needed for this base image)
#   - BR2_TARGET_GENERIC_HOSTNAME / _ISSUE (kiosk-linux branding)
#   - BR2_INIT_BUSYBOX=y                (explicit; BusyBox init is already the
#                                        Buildroot default, stated here for clarity)
#   - BR2_SYSTEM_DHCP=""                (no need for automatic eth0 DHCP on a
#                                        bare console-boot image; stock enables
#                                        it for its own networking tests)
#   - BR2_ROOTFS_OVERLAY                (adds our S99kiosk-boot-marker init script)
#   - BR2_TARGET_ROOTFS_CPIO(+_GZIP)=y / BR2_TARGET_ROOTFS_EXT2=n
#                                        (initramfs cpio.gz instead of stock's
#                                        ext2 image, per this task's interface
#                                        contract: output/images/rootfs.cpio.gz)
#   - BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_DEFCONFIG="x86_64" instead of stock's
#                                        BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_USE_CUSTOM_CONFIG +
#                                        BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_CUSTOM_CONFIG_FILE
#                                        pointing at Buildroot's own
#                                        board/qemu/x86_64/linux.config — using
#                                        the kernel's own in-tree defconfig
#                                        keeps this project independent of
#                                        Buildroot's qemu board directory
#                                        conventions, at the cost of losing a
#                                        few QEMU-specific kernel tweaks that
#                                        board/qemu/x86_64/linux.config carries
#                                        (none of which are required for a
#                                        plain serial-console boot-to-shell).
#
# BR2_PACKAGE_HOST_LINUX_HEADERS_CUSTOM_6_1 / BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_CUSTOM_VERSION*
# / BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_NEEDS_HOST_LIBELF / BR2_PACKAGE_HOST_QEMU* are dropped:
# they exist in the stock file to pin an exact kernel version and build a
# host-qemu for Buildroot's own CI test suite (runtime-tests), neither of
# which this project needs — we bring our own QEMU (test/run-qemu.sh) and are
# fine with whatever kernel version BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_DEFCONFIG="x86_64" pulls
# in via the Linux tarball Buildroot's package infrastructure selects for this
# release.

# Architecture
BR2_x86_64=y

# Toolchain
BR2_TOOLCHAIN_BUILDROOT_CXX=n

# System
BR2_TARGET_GENERIC_HOSTNAME="kiosk-linux"
BR2_TARGET_GENERIC_ISSUE="RMPG Flex Kiosk Linux (sub-project 1: base image only)"
BR2_INIT_BUSYBOX=y
BR2_SYSTEM_DHCP=""

# Rootfs overlay. Buildroot resolves BR2_ROOTFS_OVERLAY relative to $(TOPDIR)
# (Buildroot's OWN source tree), never relative to O= — a relative path here
# is fragile for exactly that reason (this was tried first and failed once
# the build moved to a Docker named-volume O= output dir with a different
# relative depth than a plain host bind mount would have had: "rsync:
# change_dir .../buildroot/../../rootfs-overlay failed: No such file or
# directory"). Use the absolute in-container path instead — build.sh always
# bind-mounts this project's kiosk-linux/ directory to /kiosk-linux, so this
# path is stable regardless of where BUILDROOT_DIR or the O= volume live.
BR2_ROOTFS_OVERLAY="/kiosk-linux/rootfs-overlay"

# Filesystem: initramfs cpio.gz, not the stock ext2 image
BR2_TARGET_ROOTFS_CPIO=y
BR2_TARGET_ROOTFS_CPIO_GZIP=y
BR2_TARGET_ROOTFS_EXT2=n
# BR2_TARGET_ROOTFS_TAR is not set

# Kernel: use the kernel's own in-tree x86_64 defconfig rather than
# Buildroot's board/qemu/x86_64/linux.config
BR2_LINUX_KERNEL=y
# LATEST_VERSION=y reads as a reproducibility smell at a glance, but it isn't
# one here: within a pinned Buildroot release (BUILDROOT_TAG), "latest" always
# resolves to the one specific kernel version that release's package
# infrastructure selects — a fresh build against the same BUILDROOT_TAG always
# pulls the same kernel.
BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_LATEST_VERSION=y
BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_DEFCONFIG="x86_64"
